xnu 0282: two finished frames wait for the tick, so a burst spreads out

One slot is not enough for a bursty renderer. Ring247 counted the records each
display tick was handed -- 8 a second got two, 8 got none, 44 got one -- and one
slot can do nothing with either extreme: the pair loses its older frame, and the
tick after it has nothing and holds the survivor for two periods. A drag then
moves in steps of 4 ms and 33 ms, which is exactly what the hand reports.

Two slots do. The older frame of a pair goes on the panel at the next tick and
the newer one at the tick after, so the burst's second frame fills the tick that
would have been empty: the panel gets one frame every period, the rate the
renderer produced, in the order it produced it.

Two is also the limit: the compositor alternates two display surfaces, so a
record held for one period is presented before the surface it names can be drawn
into again. A record that arrives with both slots full means the renderer is more
than two frames ahead; the oldest is dropped and counted as `drop` on the present
line, because the panel should show where the finger is.

The other way -- a swap that waits for the panel before returning, the way a real
display driver works -- was measured freezing this port's compositor (33 swaps in
a whole boot), so nothing here waits.

